From 8bc2626ef66735c989a9c603d2f6464b630b6aed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabe Hope <15705900+gabehope@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:54:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] Strided workgroup reductions for reductions into few
 outputs

A reduction of at least 64 elements into at most 256 outputs runs one
workgroup of 256 threads per output: thread g visits ridx = g, g + 256,
... below the reduction length (read from a uniform), then the threads
combine their partial results in workgroup memory. It works for any
reduction op, any dtype but bool, and any length, and lengths share one
shader; the existing grouped path needed float32 Add, contiguous inputs,
a length of 512+ divisible by 2-16, and used 16 threads.

Measured on an M-series GPU against one thread per output with a literal
trip count (the path these reductions took before): within ~2 us per
dispatch at 64-100 elements, 30% faster at 300-400; max of 93,250 int32
values from ~3.1 ms to under 66 us; a masked sum of 93,248 float32 values
from ~2 ms to under 66 us. (A uniform trip count in the one-thread loop
would have been 2-4x slower than either: the compiler cannot unroll it.)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
